Context: Core D-094 (removing the `same_local_endpoint_reconnect` admission bypass) is being fixed separately; the M6A tests will only go fully green after that
Core fix is packaged. Your job is the java-owned defect: an intent must be closed only by the termination of a transport that belongs to it.

## Fix (single owner)
Intent ↔ transport attribution: the raw mesh node already records the READY it admitted for an intent (connection identity + lane). A DISCONNECTED closes the
intent only when it terminates a recorded transport of that intent (same connection identity — the one the framework itself recorded from the READY it acted
on — and lane); a DISCONNECTED for an endpoint the intent never admitted must not empty its set. Do not build a new pair/generation state machine (D-086 2(b)):
use the identity the node already stores at READY. State rule count before/after (attribution rule: endpoint-key match → recorded-transport match).
Compare with dotnet `ZLinkMeshPeerAdmission`/`ZLinkManagedMeshNode` (after `3810baab93` it no longer re-filters by physical direction; how does it attribute
DISCONNECTED to an intent?) and node's mesh backend; the java rule must be the same one.

## Verify
Unit regression (foreign-lane DISCONNECTED does not close the intent; own-transport DISCONNECTED does); `ZLinkJavaRawMeshNodeM6ATest` class 3× — report per-test
results honestly (the two Core-dependent cases may still fail until D-094 is packaged; say which assertion and why); `:zlink-framework-core:test contractTest` once.

